1
00:00:01,060 --> 00:00:05,110
Suppose a student asks: do we
know the sun will rise tomorrow?

2
00:00:05,481 --> 00:00:11,471
An ordinary AI assistant can give a good
answer, often with quotations and sources.

3
00:00:11,795 --> 00:00:14,825
HumeBot can use the same kind of AI model.

4
00:00:15,220 --> 00:00:19,800
What it adds is the setting:
a Reading Desk, with Hume’s own words,

5
00:00:19,840 --> 00:00:22,690
the instructor’s reconstruction
of the argument,

6
00:00:22,730 --> 00:00:25,260
and room for students to question both.

7
00:00:27,694 --> 00:00:32,364
First, the instructor’s step-by-step
reconstruction, open to challenge.

8
00:00:32,679 --> 00:00:35,089
The sun has always risen in the past.

9
00:00:35,455 --> 00:00:37,805
Therefore, the sun will rise tomorrow.

10
00:00:38,165 --> 00:00:39,785
What premise is missing?

11
00:00:41,151 --> 00:00:45,031
One proposed answer:
the laws of nature are uniform.

12
00:00:45,388 --> 00:00:46,918
But what justifies that?

13
00:00:48,374 --> 00:00:52,474
Nature has always been uniform
in the past; therefore,

14
00:00:52,514 --> 00:00:54,454
it will be uniform in the future.

15
00:00:55,640 --> 00:01:00,510
That second inference needs the very
assumption it was meant to justify.

16
00:01:00,910 --> 00:01:03,000
The argument runs in a circle.

17
00:01:06,991 --> 00:01:11,941
Now a separate question, one HumeBot
answered in a development trial:

18
00:01:11,981 --> 00:01:17,010
what exactly, in Hume’s view, is circular
about trying to prove that the future will

19
00:01:17,050 --> 00:01:18,531
resemble the past?

20
00:01:20,968 --> 00:01:24,278
The model first works out
what an answer must cover,

21
00:01:24,318 --> 00:01:29,678
then reads all the text it has,
Sections one through nine of the Enquiry,

22
00:01:29,718 --> 00:01:31,778
and picks the passages that matter.

23
00:01:34,241 --> 00:01:37,761
The model writes its explanation
from those passages,

24
00:01:37,801 --> 00:01:39,981
though it may also draw on its training.

25
00:01:40,469 --> 00:01:44,349
AI models can misquote a text
or cite the wrong place.

26
00:01:44,731 --> 00:01:49,682
Software builds every quotation on display
from a checked transcription of Hume’s

27
00:01:49,722 --> 00:01:53,881
1777 text, and confirms its location.

28
00:01:54,351 --> 00:01:56,581
Quotations that fail are withheld.

29
00:01:57,939 --> 00:02:02,719
But the check has limits: it shows
that Hume wrote these words, here;

30
00:02:02,759 --> 00:02:07,209
not that they support the explanation,
or that the explanation is right.

31
00:02:12,187 --> 00:02:15,737
The Reading Desk brings
together two interpretations:

32
00:02:15,777 --> 00:02:19,417
the instructor’s reconstruction
and HumeBot’s explanation.

33
00:02:19,815 --> 00:02:24,985
Students can examine both interpretations
and test them against Hume’s words.

34
00:02:32,901 --> 00:02:37,721
From any quotation, a student can widen
the view: the paragraph, its neighbors,

35
00:02:37,761 --> 00:02:38,991
the whole section.

36
00:02:44,422 --> 00:02:49,432
The Enquiry can also be read on its own,
without asking HumeBot anything.

37
00:02:51,413 --> 00:02:55,163
Students can also respond to the
instructor’s reconstruction.

38
00:02:55,596 --> 00:03:00,296
When the instructor turns sharing on,
a student can attach a question,

39
00:03:00,336 --> 00:03:04,096
an objection or another reading
to one line of the argument.

40
00:03:04,538 --> 00:03:07,288
It shows who wrote it and
stays with the course,

41
00:03:07,328 --> 00:03:10,168
where classmates can read
it and add their own.

42
00:03:13,740 --> 00:03:16,660
An ordinary AI assistant gives an answer.

43
00:03:17,084 --> 00:03:22,274
HumeBot gives an answer with the means
to examine it: checked quotations,

44
00:03:22,314 --> 00:03:25,734
Hume’s surrounding text,
the instructor’s reconstruction,

45
00:03:25,774 --> 00:03:27,594
and students’ own questions.

46
00:03:27,925 --> 00:03:32,695
These are tools for reading Hume closely,
not a substitute for reading him.

47
00:03:34,058 --> 00:03:39,008
This was a development build, with a
synthetic course and a recorded answer;

48
00:03:39,048 --> 00:03:41,538
no real students have used HumeBot.
